4.2 Probability and Distributions
Normal distribution: symmetric bell curve defined by mean and standard deviation; about 68%, 95% and 99.7% of values lie within 1, 2 and 3 standard deviations.
Binomial: number of successes in n independent yes/no trials.
Poisson: count of events in a fixed interval (e.g. calls per hour).
Central Limit Theorem: the distribution of sample means approaches normal as sample size grows, whatever the underlying distribution. This underpins confidence intervals and most hypothesis tests.

4.3 Inferential Statistics
Population vs. sample: we usually observe a sample and infer something about the population.
Confidence interval: a range that would contain the true parameter in a stated percentage (e.g. 95%) of repeated samples.
Hypothesis test: the null hypothesis (H0) assumes no effect. The p-value is the probability of seeing data at least as extreme as observed if H0 is true. If p < alpha (commonly 0.05) we reject H0.
Type I / Type II errors: rejecting a true H0 (false positive) / failing to reject a false H0 (false negative).

4.4 Correlation and Regression
Pearson correlation r (from -1 to +1) measures the strength of a linear relationship. Simple linear regression fits y = b0 + b1x, and R-squared reports the share of variance in y explained by x. Correlation does not imply causation.
