Study hours show a strong positive linear relationship with score. The regression equation is score = 54.53 + 4.57 x study_hours: each additional hour of study is associated with roughly 4.6 more points, and study hours alone explain about 65% of the variance in score. Attendance has a weaker positive relationship, and the two predictors are almost uncorrelated with each other (r = 0.078), so there is no multicollinearity concern.
5.4 Hypothesis Test: Group A vs. Group B
H0: the mean score of groups A and B is equal.  H1: the means differ.
Method: Welch two-sample t-test (does not assume equal variances), alpha = 0.05.
Result: mean A = 82.05, mean B = 81.02, t = 0.682, p = 0.496.
Conclusion: p > 0.05, so we fail to reject H0. There is no statistically significant difference between the groups.

5.5 Confidence Interval
The 95% confidence interval for the mean score is [80.10, 83.03]. We can be 95% confident, in the repeated-sampling sense, that the true population mean lies in this range.
