6. Industry Best Practices
6.1 Follow a Structured Workflow
Industry teams commonly follow CRISP-DM: (1) business understanding, (2) data understanding, (3) data preparation, (4) modelling, (5) evaluation, (6) deployment. The process is iterative, and most of the time is spent on steps 2 and 3.
6.2 Data Quality and Preparation
Profile the data first: shape, dtypes, missing values, duplicates, ranges and outliers.
Handle missing data deliberately (drop, impute with mean/median/mode, or model it) and document the choice.
Validate assumptions with checks or asserts, not by eye.
Keep raw data immutable; do all cleaning in code so it can be re-run.
6.3 Reproducibility and Code Quality
Set random seeds and pin library versions (requirements.txt or environment.yml).
Use version control (Git/GitHub) with clear commit messages.
Write small functions, follow PEP 8, use meaningful names and add docstrings.
Use notebooks for exploration, and move reusable logic into scripts or modules with tests.
6.4 Sound Analysis and Modelling
Split data into train / validation / test sets before modelling to avoid data leakage.
Start with a simple baseline model, then increase complexity only if it helps.
Pick metrics that match the problem (e.g. precision/recall for imbalanced classes rather than accuracy).
Visualise data with clear titles, labelled axes and units; do not use misleading scales.
Report uncertainty (confidence intervals, p-values with effect sizes) rather than a single number.
6.5 Ethics, Privacy and Communication
Protect personal data (anonymise, minimise, follow regulations such as GDPR and India's DPDP Act).
Check for bias in data and models, and consider fairness across groups.
Communicate findings in plain language for the audience, with clear limitations and next steps.
Document assumptions, data sources and decisions so others can review the work.
7. Conclusion
This report covered the four foundations of data science: Python programming basics, data types and measurement scales, statistical foundations, and industry best practices. The practical assignment combined them in one workflow: cleaning a dataset, describing it, measuring relationships, testing a hypothesis and quantifying uncertainty. The key findings were that study hours strongly predict score (r = 0.81, R-squared = 0.65) while the two groups do not differ significantly (p = 0.50). Together these fundamentals form the base for more advanced work such as machine learning, data engineering and deployment.
